::STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A study finds students who spend more time reading tend to have higher vocabulary scores. What conclusion is safest?

{
=A. Reading time is associated with vocabulary score#Correct. This describes the relationship without claiming the study proves cause and effect.
~B. Reading time always causes higher vocabulary scores#This overstates the evidence. Association alone does not prove causation.
~C. Vocabulary score causes students to read more#This reverses the claim and still assumes causation without evidence.
~D. There is no relationship because the variables are different#Different variables can still be associated when a pattern appears.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
When screen time increases, sleep time tends to decrease. What type of association is described?

{
~A. Positive association#Positive association means both variables tend to increase together.
=B. Negative association#Correct. One variable increases while the other tends to decrease.
~C. No association#There is a pattern, so no association is not the best description.
~D. Proven causation#A pattern does not automatically prove cause and effect.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A chart shows people who own gym memberships exercise more often. Does the chart alone prove the membership caused the exercise?

{
~A. Yes, because the two variables are related#A relationship alone does not prove causation.
~B. Yes, because exercise is healthy#This may be true, but it does not prove a causal claim from the chart.
=C. No, because another factor could explain the association#Correct. Motivation, schedule, or income could affect both membership and exercise.
~D. No, because charts cannot show any association#Charts can show association, but association is not the same as causation.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Two variables have no clear pattern in a scatterplot. What conclusion is appropriate?

{
~A. One variable caused the other#Causation is not supported, especially when there is no clear pattern.
~B. The variables have a strong positive association#A strong positive association would show an upward pattern.
~C. The variables have a strong negative association#A strong negative association would show a downward pattern.
=D. There is no clear association shown#Correct. No visible pattern means no clear association is supported.
}

::STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A news headline says, "Eating breakfast causes higher grades" based only on a survey. What word should replace causes?

{
=A. is associated with#Correct. A survey can show a relationship, but it does not automatically prove cause and effect.
~B. eliminates#This changes the meaning and is not supported by the survey.
~C. guarantees#Statistical relationships do not guarantee an outcome for every person.
~D. prevents#This is a causal claim and is not supported by the survey alone.
}
